artifact :the_seven_line_card do
  name "The Seven-Line Card"
  aka "七行", "a card"
  tags :agriculture, :legibility, :locality, :trade
  prominence :marginal

  prose <<~PROSE
    A hand's-width card of pressed rag paper carrying a variety's name, a number, and seven lines. Each line names one character a hand or a mouth can judge without an issued instrument, and each line carries a range and the proportion of a bed allowed to fall outside it. A card with six lines is a note. A card with eight has been argued over and the eighth is somebody's addition.

    Cards are numbered by the season they were entered and the bed that proved them, so two cards standing under one name are told apart by their numbers rather than by which of them is right. A #{ref :the_grow_out, "proving bed"} reads a lot against one numbered card and files the sheet under that number.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :description, heading: "The Card For Èrjīngtiáo"
    The most widely held card in either tradition describes 二荆条, a long thin-walled pepper grown from Sichuan to Baja. Its lines run: days from transplant to the first colour break; pod length at the break, against a notched stick; wall thickness at the shoulder, split with a thumbnail; whether the pod hooks, and to which side; heat at the tip set against heat at the shoulder; what a crushed ripe pod smells of; and how a pod dries on a string — papery and hollow, or leathery and staying full.

    Six of the seven are judged in a single afternoon. The seventh takes six weeks on the string and is the reason a bed cannot close before the autumn.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "The Tin And The Chain"
    A card without its tin settles nothing. The tin is a sealed portion of the seed the card was written from, kept dry at the bench and opened only to sow a proving bed's middle rows.

    A tin is refilled from the bed it proved, and every refill is entered on the back of the card with the year and the bed. A card's authority is that chain. A card whose chain broke — a tin emptied, spoiled or lost, and refilled from whatever seed was to hand — is marked loose, stays in the book, and cannot be cited against a lot.

    Each refill is also a generation grown somewhere. The #{ref :the_saints_of_plenty, "Saints"} refill from the driest bed their settlements can water; the #{ref :the_garden_courts, "Garden Courts"} refill from the valley. The èrjīngtiáo card's chain runs to four refills on one side and eleven on the other, and the two tins are no longer sown against each other. #{ref :the_seventh_line, "The Seventh Line"} is the bed on which the difference was read.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — The card is worthless without a sealed reference portion, and the portion is regenerated rather than consumed, from seed-certification practice around standard samples: authority rests on an unbroken chain of regenerations, each recorded. That decided the drift this object produces — every refill is a generation grown in one place, so two benches keeping the same card diverge by keeping it correctly."
end
